      "retrieved": "2026-10-17",
      "revision": 1368940815,
      "text": "Dichelostemma congestum is a species of flowering plant known by the common name ookow or fork-toothed ookow. It is native to California, Oregon and Washington."
    },
    "es": {
      "source": "Wikipedia (es): Dichelostemma congestum",
      "source_url": "https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dichelostemma_congestum",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-17",
      "revision": 174388057,
      "text": "Dichelostemma congestum es una planta herbácea, perenne y bulbosa perteneciente de la subfamilia Brodiaeoideae de las asparagáceas. Es nativa de las colinas y montañas del oeste de Estados Unidos."
    }
  },
  "sections": {
    "uses": {
      "en": {
        "source": "Wikipedia (en): Dichelostemma congestum § Uses",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dichelostemma_congestum#Uses",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-17",
        "revision": 1368940815,
        "heading": "Uses",
        "text": "The bulbs of D. congestum can be consumed and their use is part of the ethnobotany of multiple indigenous people of California. The bulbs were eaten raw by the Maidu and Nisenan who, along with the Hupa, also further prepared the bulbs for consumption by roasting them in ashes or boiling them in baskets.",
        "truncated": false
      }
    }
